solution:
The domain of a function is the set of all possible input values (x-values) for which the function produces a real output value.

The expression $z(x) = \sqrt[3]{x - 1} + \sqrt[3]{8 - x}$ involves two cube roots, and we can take the cube root of any real number.

However, to ensure the expression remains real and is defined, we must have:
   \begin{align*}
   x - 1 &amp; \in \mathbb{R} \\
   8 - x &amp; \in \mathbb{R}
   \end{align*}

Since both $x - 1$ and $8 - x$ are linear expressions in terms of $x$, they are real for all real $x$.

Therefore, the domain of the function $z(x) = \sqrt[3]{x - 1} + \sqrt[3]{8 - x}$ is the set of all real numbers:
\[ \boxed{(-\infty, \infty)} \]

