solution:
To evaluate this determinant, use the cofactor expansion along the first column:
\begin{align*}
\begin{vmatrix} 1 & x & y \\ 1 & x + y & y \\ 1 & x & x + y \end{vmatrix} &= 1 \begin{vmatrix} x + y & y \\ x & x + y \end{vmatrix} - 1 \begin{vmatrix} x & y \\ x & x + y \end{vmatrix} + 1 \begin{vmatrix} x & y \\ x + y & y \end{vmatrix}
\end{align*}

Calculate each of these $2 \times 2$ determinants:
\begin{align*}
\begin{vmatrix} x + y & y \\ x & x + y \end{vmatrix} &= (x+y)^2 - xy \\
&= x^2 + 2xy + y^2 - xy \\
&= x^2 + xy + y^2
\end{align*}

\begin{align*}
\begin{vmatrix} x & y \\ x & x + y \end{vmatrix} &= x(x + y) - xy \\
&= x^2 + xy - xy \\
&= x^2
\end{align*}

\begin{align*}
\begin{vmatrix} x & y \\ x + y & y \end{vmatrix} &= xy - (x + y)y \\
&= xy - xy - y^2 \\
&= -y^2
\end{align*}

Now substitute these results back into the expansion:
\begin{align*}
\begin{vmatrix} 1 & x & y \\ 1 & x + y & y \\ 1 & x & x + y \end{vmatrix} &= 1(x^2 + xy + y^2) - 1(x^2) + 1(-y^2) \\
&= x^2 + xy + y^2 - x^2 - y^2 \\
&= \boxed{xy}
\end{align*}

