solution:
Group the terms as: $\left(\frac{5}{3} \times \frac{15}{15}\right)\times\left(\frac{6}{10} \times \frac{12}{20} \times \frac{24}{40}\right)\times\left(\frac{9}{9}\times\frac{18}{30}\right)\times\left(\frac{25}{21} \times \frac{35}{30}\right)$

Then calculate each group separately and multiply them:
\begin{align*}
   \frac{5}{3} \times \frac{15}{15} = 1 \qquad & \left( \frac{6}{10} \times \frac{12}{20} \times \frac{24}{40} \right) = \frac{6}{10} \times \frac{6}{10} \times \frac{6}{10} = \left( \frac{3}{5} \right)^3 = \frac{27}{125} \\ \\
   \left( \frac{9}{9} \times \frac{18}{30} \right) = \frac{3}{5} \qquad & \left( \frac{25}{21} \times \frac{35}{30} \right) = \frac{35}{21} \times \frac{35}{42} = \left( \frac{5}{3} \times \frac{7}{6} \right)^2 = \frac{1225}{1296}
\end{align*}

After simplifying each group, calculate the final product: $1 \times \frac{27}{125} \times \frac{3}{5} \times \frac{1225}{1296} = \boxed{1}$.

