label("$D$",D,ESE);[/asy]

solution:
First we will find $\mathbf{A} \mathbf{B}$ and $\mathbf{B} \mathbf{A}$.
\begin{align*}
\mathbf{A} \mathbf{B} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 2 \\ 3 & 4 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} a & b \\ c & d \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} a + 2c & b + 2d \\ 3a + 4c & 3b + 4d \end{pmatrix} \\
\mathbf{B} \mathbf{A} = \begin{pmatrix} a & b \\ c & d \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 2 \\ 3 & 4 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} a + 3b & 2a + 4b \\ c + 3d & 2c + 4d \end{pmatrix}
\end{align*}

Since $\mathbf{A} \mathbf{B} = \mathbf{B} \mathbf{A}$ we know that corresponding elements in each product matrix are equal. Equate the elements in the first row of each matrix:
\begin{align*}
a + 2c &= a + 3b \\
2c &= 3b \\
\frac{2}{3} c &= b \\
\end{align*}

Equate the elements in the second row of each matrix:
\begin{align*}
3a + 4c &= c + 3d \\
3a + 3c &= 3d \\
a + c &= d \\
a - d &= -c \\
\end{align*}

Thus,
\[ \frac{a - d}{c - 3b} = \frac{-c}{c - 3 \left( \frac{2}{3} c \right)} = \frac{-c}{c - 2c} = \frac{-c}{-c} = \boxed{1} \]

