solution:
To solve this inequality, we'll consider the sign of the expression $(2x-7)(x-3)/x$ for different intervals of $x$.

Note that the expression is undefined at $x=0$ (since division by zero is not allowed), and it changes sign at $x=3$ and $x=7/2$.

We can create a sign table as follows:

\begin{align*}
\begin{array}{c|c|c|c}
x&-\infty&\infty\\ \hline
(x-3) &-&+&\\
(2x-7)&-&-&+\\
x&-&+&\\
\dfrac{(2x-7)(x-3)}{x}&+&-&+\\
\end{array}
\begin{array}{c}
\Bigg\vert \; 0\\
\Bigg\vert \; 3 \\
\Bigg\vert \; \dfrac{7}{2}
\end{array}
\end{align*}

Since we want the expression to be non-negative, the solution is the intervals where the expression is positive or zero.

Looking at the sign table, we see that the expression is positive in the intervals $x<0$ and $x>7/2$, and it is zero at $x=3$ and $x=7/2$.

So, the solution in interval notation is:
\[ \boxed{(0, 3] \cup [\tfrac72, \infty)} \]

