solution:
Since $(\triangle+q)+q=\triangle+2q$, we have that $\triangle+2q=106$.  Also, we are given that $\triangle+q=59$.  Subtracting this from $\triangle+2q=106$, we get
\begin{align*}
(\triangle+2q)-(\triangle+q)&=106-59\\
\Rightarrow\qquad q&=47.
\end{align*}Plugging this value of $q$ into the equation $\triangle+q=59$, we find
\begin{align*}
\triangle+47&=59\\
\Rightarrow\qquad \triangle&=\boxed{12}.
\end{align*}

