solution:
We need to find the coefficient of the $x^2$ term in the product. To do this, we need to multiply each term of the first expression with each term of the second expression and identify the $x^2$ terms.

Expanding the product:
\begin{align*}
(2x^2 + 3x + 4)(5x^2 + 6x + 7) = \,
& 2x^2(5x^2) + 2x^2(6x) + 2x^2(7) + \\
& 3x(5x^2) + 3x(6x) + 3x(7) + \\
& 4(5x^2) + 4(6x) + 4(7)
\end{align*}

Simplifying the expansion:
\begin{align*}
10x^4 + 12x^3 + 14x^2 + 15x^3 + 18x^2 + 21x + 20x^2 + 24x + 28
\end{align*}

Combining like terms:
\begin{align*}
& 10x^4 + (12x^3 + 15x^3) + (14x^2 + 18x^2 + 20x^2) + (21x + 24x) + 28 \\
& 10x^4 + 27x^3 + 52x^2 + 45x + 28
\end{align*}

The coefficient of the $x^2$ term is $\boxed{52}$. \\

